Dan Bethe <dtm@hpnc.com> wrote:

> I've scoured the PGCC and EGCS web sites up and down, and read the
> distribution docs.  Never used it except for on Pentium.  It seems to
> have uniqueness compared to GCC, from what the docs say.  You guys use
> it on Alphas? 

> On another thread I still don't understand the difference between EGCS
> and PGCC except that they're maintained by different groups.  Hmm.

PGCC is an effort to improve the performance of generated code by gcc on 
Pentia.  I don't even know if it scores much better than plain GCC on Pentium 
Pro's and Pentium II's.

EGCS is an effort to include into a GCC-like distribution new features that 
were determined to be too *experimental* by the current GCC maintainer.  Note 
that the features are deemed experimental; the releases should be on par with 
GCC wrt stability.

Although a lot of improvements in PGCC were brought into EGCS, not all are.  
One thing that's lacking is complete "correct" alignment of all `double' data 
- static as well as automatic (stack based).  The hard problem is to do it in 
a way that doesn't break binary compatibility with existing (Intel) ABI's.

Of course, the last thing is hardly important to Alpha users, although I'm 
not entirely convinced yet that we don't face a 128-bit alignment issue - at 
least the current Alpha Architecture Manual touts that as being beneficial.

HTH,

