According to Paul English <tallpaul@mathworks.com>:
>Jon Fiedler (jmf9@po.cwru.edu) wrote:
><snip>
>: than when I bought mine), and the system is nice enough.  The only
>: problem I've had is finding cache for it (I haven't looked all that
>: hard, but if anyone knows a source, I'd be interested in finding out).
>: Even without cache, the performance is acceptable for me (I'm running
>: RedHat 4.2).  Just make sure you buy true parity RAM (x32 RAM will NOT
>: work).
>
>True Parity? Will ECC work? I'm a little confused on the difference
>between the two (if any) anyway...

72 pin ECC SIMMs will not work (AFAIK) since they do error correction on
the SIMM.  The Alpha motherboards use a 64 bit memory bus (at least),
and when you have standard parity SIMMs, with 64 bits of RAM you have 8
parity bits.  Well, the normal single bit correct/double bit detect ECC
algorithm needs 8 bits to work on 64 bits of memory, so the motherboard
uses the extra (parity) bits to implement ECC instead of parity.

The reason you need what is called "true" parity RAM is that some people
sell "logic" parity RAM that instead of having a true extra bit for
every 8 bits, they have logic to generate an even or odd (I forget
which) bit when the parity bit is read and ignore writes to it.  That is
fine for computers that just use the bit for parity, but when you try to
write ECC information, it gets tossed and you don't get the correct
values back.

In short, get "true" parity RAM, not "logic" parity or ECC RAM.
-- 
Chris Adams - cadams@ro.com
System Administrator - Renaissance Internet Services
I don't speak for anybody but myself - that's enough trouble.



