I don't think that's right.  I forget what the difference is exactly
but I think it has something to do with row/column addressing.  The
error correction is still done by the motherboard chipset.

|	     The Alpha motherboards use a 64 bit memory bus (at least),
| and when you have standard parity SIMMs, with 64 bits of RAM you have 8
| parity bits.  Well, the normal single bit correct/double bit detect ECC
| algorithm needs 8 bits to work on 64 bits of memory, so the motherboard
| uses the extra (parity) bits to implement ECC instead of parity.

Right.



