jum@anubis.han.de (Jens-Uwe Mager) writes:
 
> I hope so. I was just pointing out that munging ones return address is not
> to be considered spam fighting.

I agree with this 100%.  IMO, the 'munging addresses' syndrome is as
much expressive of a sheeplike mentality as anything else.  (see
below).  'Everybody else is doing it, I'm gonna do it, too.'  It's
clearly being done mostly by individuals with little sense of how the
mail & news systems work.  It's not effective in reducing the load on
the system -- this is my biggest gripe about it.

Most mail servers now require an resolvable Sender: line before
they'll accept mail.  This may be the future for news, too.  This may
make it harder for some of us who have linux boxes to get into
newsgroups.  But in the long run, it will make for a more coherent
system -- people have to learn to be responsible for their own
behavior.  Including their behavior in the face of spam.

I just looked at the headers of several messages in this group with
'munged' addresses in them.  Ironically, every one of these messages
had the correct address of the originating system in it somewhere.
I'm sure that for an experienced programmer/script-writer, it would be
trivially easy to recreate one or more correct addresses from these
headers.  The likelihood is that they'll create a half-dozen addresses
and spam all of them -- thus greatly increasing the amount of
bandwidth wasted.  Munging addresses not only is 'not fighting spam'
-- it's counterproductive.

mp

