jum@anubis.han.de (Jens-Uwe Mager) writes:

> I hope so. I was just pointing out that munging ones return address is not
> to be considered spam fighting.

I've never said it was.  What I've said is that I use it *in addition*
to spam fighting.

You've put it to me that my munging does not deter the spammers, and
that I am a fool for thinking so.

SO, I decided to sacrifice myself and made a few postings without
munging.  I made a few in soc.culture.japan, and I saw my spam count
increase somewhat.  I then posted in a few alt.* groups, and I saw
my spam count explode!

Thanks Jens-Uwe.  Thanks Kai.  My mailbox is now receiving so much
spam it has been rendered useless.  To hell with you.

I've now been completely convinced that munging *is* an effective
deterrent, and have no intention of changing that practice until better
solutions are available to me.  Those of you who wish to complain to my
postmaster, about it, feel free, but he is likely to laugh at your
complaint.

Sure, the odd spammer will get past my munged address.  And I will
deal with those thieves with extreme prejudice.  But the point is, a
good portion of spam *is* cut down by munging and that is good enough
for me.

You know, munging is more than a slight inconvenience for me.  You
have to remove "FOOBAR" from my address to contact me.  Myself, I don't
have any software that lets me mung (Tin doesn't support it but I
do not wish to consider any other software than Tin) so I have to:

- Save any article I want to follow-up to a file.
- Edit the file, changing or adding headers by hand (for a new article,
I read in a template of headers and then hand customize as needed).
- Copy the article to my "screen" buffer.
- Telnet to a news server.
- Paste the article from my "screen" buffer.

This is quite a bit more inconvenient than pressing "f" to followup,
or "w" to post from my favorite news reader!  But the benefits to my
mailbox far outway the hassles.

To those who would condemn me for munging:  TO HELL WITH YOU!

