John Wilson wrote:
> 
> In article <6c7ohq$slu$1@nntp6.u.washington.edu>,
> M. Oesterwinter <marcuso@saul1.u.washington.edu> wrote:
> >Cheapest I found at JDR is $9.99.  Does this mean that it will cost $110
> >to put 1 meg cache in a system?
> 
> That's right.  I've heard that it's worth it but I never ran my machine
> with 256 KB cache so I don't know how much difference it really makes.

This is about the fourth time I've posted this information;
it probably should go in a FAQ somewhere.

For cheap AXPpci33 cache chips, try Alliance Semiconductor:

http://www.alsc.com
http://www.alsc.com/product/sram.html

The AXPpci33 manual (August 1995 revision, does anyone know of a
later one?) lists Alliance part AS7C1024 (128Kx8 SRAM) as acceptable
for 1Mbyte cache:

http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/axppci/design_guide.ps.Z

A PDF datasheet is available at the Alliance site above. This
includes a list of distributors. The local distributor quoted me
$3.95CDN each for the AS7C1024-15PC, the 15ns 32-pin DIP part.
The 32Kx8 part, AS7C256-15PC, is going for about $1.00CDN.
This works fine in my AXPpci33, although it's not listed in the
manual. The pinout seems to be standard.

A slight complication is that the AXPpci33 seems to require
the 400mil wide 128Kx8 part, but these are apparently no longer
available from Alliance. After examining the pinouts on the spec
sheets, it appears to me that the 300mil 128Kx8 part will work
if you bend out pins 30, 31, and 32 so that they go in the 400mil
sockets instead of the 300mil holes. The other pins which are
common to the 128Kx8 (32 pin) and 32Kx8 (28 pin) parts have
identical assignments. I haven't actually tried this yet, so
satisfy yourself that it's correct before doing it.


Ian Willmott
