jmfix@hotmail.com wrote:
::> Greetings!!
::> I've just bought The Complete Red Hat LINUX Operating System ver. 4.2, and I
::> have a few questions about it.  If you can give me any help,
::> recommendations, or where to look for, I'll appreciate it.

Welcome to Linux land

::> The questions are the following.

::> 1-  Are there any Win95 programs that I can run on Linux??
::>         for example: ICQ (by Mirabilis)(There is a JAVA version of ICQ, I
::> don't know if it would work on Linux), games, apps, etc.

It should work.  if my memory is correct, I heard that a Linux ICQ has 
been developing.

::> 2-  Can I have Linux and Win95 on the same computer??

Yes. 

::>         Let's say, that I have a few Win95 programs, that I cannot run under
::> Linux, etc.  Or better yet, can I create a menu to choose between, using Linux
::> and Win95??  Do I need to have different partitions to run them??

Yes, you can make a menu to choose to boot into which OSs.  It can be 
done by using LILO or using LOADLIN in DOS's boot menu

You don't have to have different partitions to run Lunix and Win95, but 
you *DO* want to.  One way to allow them to share partition is to use 
umsdos filesystem, which will probably slow down the system.  Given that 
hard disk is cheap.  Put them in two partitions.

::> 3-  Where can I download programs or updates for Linux?

Yes. for RH, www.redhat.com

::> 4-  Do I need to re-partition my HD's??
::>         I have 2 HD's, one 3.1GB partitioned in half as C: and E:.  The second
::> HD(420MB), is partitioned as D: and F:.  Both HD's partitions were created
::> using DOS 6.22 FDISK.  Do I need to use Linux partition utility??  I don't

You do need to use Linux's fdisk to modify the partition type to Linux's 
file system.   By doing so, you will lose a DOS partition and it may 
cause drive letter change!!!  I hate that.. Damn Microsoft

::> mind if I have to start my computer from scratch again, after all, I have done
::> it a Trillion times with Win95 <smile>.

Okay.  If you want to, you can remove all partitions and make a primary 
partition for Linux, one for Windows.
Oh yeah.. You do need to have a swap parititon as well.

::> 5-  I have the following peripherals, are there any Linux drivers for them??
::>         Iomega ZIP Drive (PP), Play's Snappy Video Capture, Motorola
::> ModemSURFR 33.6 (external), HP DeskJet 672C Printer, Logitech MouseMan+, CROSS
::> IPen Tablet, Memorex CRW-1622 CD-Burner, a 16X CD-ROM and a Diamond Viper 330
::> 2D/3D video card (4MB).

ZIP drv, modem , printer are not a problem.
The CRW... not sure.
You may need to check www.xfree86.org to see if your Diamond card is 
supported.

 ::> 6-  What about connecting to the internet using Linux??

That's easy.  Just browse the HOWTOs in /usr/doc

