There are very definate pros and cons to running NT at all, if you're a
multimedia guy.
I'm running a P2 266 with NT, and will be shortly changing over to a 164PC
(running Linux this
time around).

NT has some good things going for it: fully 32-bit, and has a nicer overall
interface than 95 IMO.
However, the NT API design will cause problems with some games, particularly
DOS-based
games looking for Soundblaster. They can't access the sound card information
quite properly.
But, Direct X games all run fine, since they're designed to use an API anyway.
Also, if you run
NTFS you'll run into problems with DOS games on occasion.

Now, Alpha NT has a large number of programs designed to run natively on it. I
don't believe there
is any kind of speed hit to them. DEC also has an x86 emulator called FX!32,
which lets you run
x86 NT and 95 games on it. However, I think it runs them slower, somewhere
around a PPro 200.

So, think it over. Might want to look over the DEC site: www.alpha.digital.com.
They have information
on the NT applications for Alpha. But the SPECint95 and SPECfp95 seem to show
that a 164PC
533 will run faster than even a P2 333, so with the emulator you still might be
doing fine.

