Let me offer something of an explanation/defense for the current alpha
market from the consumer point of view:

Yes, DEC could have tried harder to market the alpha, there should few
people to dispute this(including the stockholders), but there may be some
logic behind that(perhaps outdated?) that might help people trying to
make the decision you see below("is the alpha for me?".

(This is just my opinion, BUT)

As it is, the alpha has one nagging problem when it comes to use in
the"mass" market":

It strives to be, and usually is, the cutting edge of processor
technology.  I know they have GHz mockups and one offs hiding in the lab,
but lets face it, the market for SDRAM that actually runs at even a
paultry 100MHz :) is just getting on its feet.	That is just one of the
more annoying things you find when trying to setup an alpha in your
garage(not that you should ever relegate that beutifull alpha to the
garage!)

This means that it will only be fully compatable with the "latest" of most
everything.

Sure modems are modems and sound cards are sound cards, but I have been
surprised to see just how many PCI video cards the 164LX spits back at
you for various reasons.

Then you have the next facit of this in software compatibility: DEC does
not have the funding or interest to have "evangelists" selling the latest
MS software idea(boy is that backwards or what?  I thought the Hardware
software equation went the other way?!?).

So, for the most part software designed for the Alpha has been limited to
what will run and what people want to run under digital UNIX
(engineering, video editing, workstation, server applications).

Linux has changed this picture substantially, but even this does not give
you full access to the "PC" software market.  Nt as well, but in a far
less impressive(32bit) manner.

Yes FX32 will let you run just about anything that will run under NT, but
you pay a horrible performance price for that emulation(even after the
optimization it is a shame to see the alpha working so hard for so little
benifit (running office is so thankless for a CPU :))

Some things may run faster than on a PII, but not without exception and
not without getting some visits from the Dr. Watson (the windows way of
saying "Thank you, drive through!")

BUT...

The alpha exists for one purpose - to move bits from here to there as
fast as any doped silicon can.

It is a purebread meant for speed.  Much like a dragster with a lopey
camshaft, it is not always the most efficient thing to putter from light
to light in city traffic.  Not always the most pleasent thing to sit in
traffic either (those of you with such cars[no mufflers, 500Hp, carpet
removed etc..] know what I am talking about).

It would be fabulous if poeple would get together and start writing games
for this beast, they would be impressive (I think you shall see this with
intel stamping out alphas at thier new plant!).

But they have not done this YET!!

SO, SHOULD JOE WIN95 user go out and buy an alpha cause its its integer
and floating point performance put a pentium 333 to shame?

ONLY, IF JOE WIN95 user knows what he/she is getting into.  It is a
beutifull thing to see your code rip through a set of calculations 4
times faster than your PPRO 200 (watch out for large exponents though
:)).

But it is not quake at 100fps!! (not that much more than 30 is
perceptable, but you get my point).

The point being that the alpha is great fun for people who like speed for
the sake of speed.  People who like to get thier hands dirty in the code
(NT is not much use here, I am talking abou linux).  People who intend on
being those who wrote the first set of commercial video games exclusively
for the alpha.

The reason you are getting the brush off from so many people selling
alpha's, is that tech support is expensive and setting up alphas has not
been refined to the degree that intel based hardware has.

The supported hardware list is shorter.  The drivers in any given OS are
harder to find, the code is newer (read: more bugs :))	Sometime you just
have to sit down and hack some code to get the results you want.

There are vendors out there willing to help you out such as:

www.dcginc.com	(I have plugged them enough, do I get a discount?
Steve...? :)

We should recognize those people and ensure that they are around to sell
us the next round o lightning fast hardware (next month? :))

But most are still focused on finding someone else to deal with the
support problems that go with selling cutting edge hardware.

I hope this does not scare anyone away.  I knew this going into it and I
have had a great time getting this to run, scouring the newsgroups,
writing a little code here and there.  More users/owners can only
increase the markets desire to make the alpha a commonplace thing in the
homes of gaming speedfreaks everywhere.

Shortly, we should see enough market base that the alpha will be as
useable and freindly as anything else out there.  Those who have had some
time may even have thier alphas tweaked to the point that the wife has no
idea what OS she is in when she steals the computer on the weekend to
surf.  It is not an issue of the hardware being more problematic, just
the software being less available.

BTW: Tom of tom's hardware guide(sysdoc.pair.com) has a PII 333 running
at 416MHz without troubles (he reports).  This would be a better choice
for a game machine at this point in time!  (slight drool on my part, but
alas the cash is spent! for the time being)  I am holding out on the true
100MHz(dechutes) bus chips from intel before I throw any more cash that
direction!

Thoughts, counterpoints, contradictions, corrections to my horrible
spell'n?

/Mike

