Although that seems logical, it was not the case when we asked
DEC one time (although it was years ago, and their policy may
have changed).

> Here at work, 1 person could of bought the CD's and
> 1001 people could use them to install
> Digital Unix on there individual platforms but first
> they have to pay for the license to use it.

Absolutely!  The rules are entirely different within a single organization.
DEC encourages people to get a single central subscription and use it to
update multiple machines.

For new machine purposes DEC says that every organization should have
a copy of the CD-ROMs available somewhere, but that is not so much a
legal requirement regarding rights to use the software as a logistic
requirement if the machines are to be supported.  It is legal to buy
a new machine with preloaded software and never get a copy on CD-ROM,
but betting on the infallibility of computers in retaining data is
something not done by those whose computing experience measures longer
than 2 weeks.

Larry Kilgallen



