Well, actually, you can compiler old libc5 code just fine under RedHat
5.0, and I've found very little code that won't compile properly under
Glibc. It's probably a good thing that they are moving to Glibc, since
this is the ultimate goal for Linux as a whole (the idea is to move to
Glibc in a manner to the transition for AOUT to ELF years back). GLIBC
has substantial advantages as far as thread support, proper POSIX
reentrant calls, etc. -- this makes many pacakages that were difficult
to port to Linux much easier.


>This is actually against the spirit of Linux.  Linus said something to
>the effect that he always wanted to tweak Linux to work with existing
>software, not force coders to write specifically for Linux or to code a
>certain way.  Programs that once compiled on many flavors of Unix now
>won't compile on Linux with Redhat's ideals of "making things better". 

I find the exact opposite to be true. GLIBC almost universally provides
better compatibility with commercial UNIXes. GLIBC is far more portable
than libc5 in most every respect. If POSIX compatibility is key, then
GLIBC is a far better product. The fact that libc5 left out a good
amount of standard POSIX functionality was bad -- but it also permitted
programmers to get sloppy and write Linux-specific code that was not
portable to other systems. GLIBC helps eliminate these problems.


>They definitely didn't make it better (it was fine before) -- although
>if they wanted to increase dependency on their .rpm's they definitely
>did it -- by breaking the compiler!

The compiler hasn't changed one iota.


>If Redhat wants to go against the spirit of Linux, they should write
>their own OS.  Don't let them fool you into thinking this is how Linux
>should be.

Red Hat is far from going against the spirit of Linux. The transition
to the more portable GLIBC library has been in the works for some time
now. You will soon see the other distributions following suit, particularly
when the next major kernel revision comes out.




