Get the facts straight, dammit.

1. RedHat had absolutelly nothing to do with the decision to switch to
   libc.  the decision had already been made months before that libc5 would
   be dropped, there would be no more development of it, and that glibc
   would be replacing libc5 as the primary linux c library.

2. more than likely, all those old programs that compiled everywhere, and
   now just don't compile on linux, did so because all of the linux
   specific hacks, primarily the #include <linux/*>'s where wrapped in
   #ifdef LINUX's.  now, those programs should no longer need them.  this
   means that there will be a certain amount of work to port over existing
   programs (work which RedHat and debian are nice enough to do the bulk
   of!!), but future programs will require that much less work to compile
   cleanly on multiple platforms including linux.

3. they didn't do anything to the compiler, except link it to the new
   library.  they're still going with 2.7.2.3, considered to be stable. 

4. the person here was asking about alpha's.  well, if you look for libc5
   on alpha's, you'll be sorely disapointed - AFAIK, it *never* compiled on
   alpha platforms.  and, RedHat, the only distro I know of to carry an
   alpha version, has always released it with glibc.  some standard, eh?

