>
>Somehow my root password has 'become corrupted' so I have no privileged account
>on this machine.  I cannot do a controlled shutdown from any of my
>non-privileged accounts, nor can I edit the password file.  I hoped to do a
>single-user startup so I can remedy the situation - I imagined that there might
>be a suitable MILO boot-string to pass to the kernel for this.  Is there?
>
>I don't want to shutdown and 'try it out' at the moment, because this machine
>acts as a mail server for some of our incoming email, and is set to restart
>without the network interfaces activated ... no root, no networks, no mail!

Something like:

MILO> boot sda1:/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda1 single

should give you a sulogin prompt, but you still need to enter the root
password. Sulogin does do a good job in finding out it the root password,
or the password file, has been corrupted and skips the password: prompt
if so (at least it accepts everything as a password then).

Another option is:

MILO> boot sda1:/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda1 init=/bin/sh

Which starts up a shell instead of init. You'll have to remount root rw
(mount -n -oremount,rw) at that point and then you can edit the password
file. Remount the root file system ro after that and reboot.

Mike.

